; m5-clockout-cloop-dell.run — the clock-out CAMD proof on the Dell (ABIv11).
;
; Run with: spike_server.py submit --run-script RAM:M5-CLOOP.RUN
; NOT with --exec: the lane's --exec is not a shell, and one blocked command
; there wedges the agent's whole command path until an owner reboot.
;
; COMMENTS ARE ';'. Measured on the riqemu1 lane across all five candidates:
; '#', 'REM' and '/*' are each executed and each print "object not found".
;
; WHY THIS RUN EXISTS. The riqemu1 run proved COUNTS -- 1000 sent, 1000
; received, 0 lost -- but reported verdict=2 JITTERY, which is that lane's
; lack of real-time pacing plus batch draining, not wire behaviour. The Dell
; paces for real, so the same code here produces intervals that mean
; something. This is the lane that can answer the timing question.
;
; Same shape as the riqemu1 script: both ends are the two TASKS of one
; process, because one lane runs one script at a time and `cmd &` does not
; background here.

echo M5 clock loopback on the Dell starting
RAM:MIDISEND RIAPP CLOCKLOOP 60 RAM:CLOOPD.LOG
type RAM:CLOOPD.LOG
echo M5 clock loopback on the Dell done